course: dictionary
lesson: 3196
title: Endure: bear it, or last
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word endure has two meanings, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. Look at what the sentence is about: is someone facing something hard, or is something lasting a long time?
explain: «Truthful lips endure for ever, but a lying tongue lasts only a moment» (Proverbs 12:19). The clue is the contrast: truth endures for ever, but a lie lasts only a moment. So endure must mean last for a long time.
explain: The other meaning is to bear something hard or painful without giving up. If a runner keeps going through the pain of a long race, she endures it. Check by swapping in another word: if "bore" or "kept going through" makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: To endure means to bear or suffer something hard or painful without giving up.
fact: To endure also means to last for a long time.
fact: «Truthful lips endure for ever» (Proverbs 12:19) uses the second meaning: the truth lasts.
fact: A good check is to swap in last or bear and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Endure is a verb, so it changes: endures, endured, enduring.
exercise: «The soldiers endured the freezing night without complaint.» What does endured mean here? | Fell asleep at once | *Bore the hardship without giving up | Ran away from the cold | Left the camp early
exercise: «Her love for her sister would endure for ever.» What does endure mean here? | Change its colour | Be forgotten by everyone | Fade away quickly | *Last for a long time
exercise: In «Truthful lips endure for ever», which word could replace endure? | Rush | Shout | Hide | *Last
test: «She endured the pain of the blister and finished the walk.» What does endured mean here? | *Bore it without giving up | Forgot it | Enjoyed it | Ran from it
test: Which word is closest in meaning to endure, meaning to last? | Break | *Remain | Vanish | Shrink
exam: Which sentence uses endure to mean last for a long time? | He endured the cold wind on the hill | The runner endured the pain of the race | We endured a very long wait | *Her memories of the holiday will endure for years
